    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Women’s community sport in Iran concerns opportunities for regular recreational activity, social exercise and participation close to home. Research identifies interacting constraints involving time, affordability, facilities and social expectations, alongside the encouragement that friends and family can provide.",
    "tags": ["Women’s sport", "Community recreation", "Physical activity", "Public space", "Access", "Social participation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:40:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:40:04Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Women’s Community Sport in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore research on Iranian women’s everyday recreational activity, including social support, time pressures, facility access and local differences."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "everyday-participation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Sport as an everyday opportunity",
        "content": "Community sport concerns the possibility of taking part regularly, rather than reaching a national team. Research on Iranian women’s physical activity offers a useful window into that possibility, including walking with companions, using local facilities and attending exercise sessions. Physical activity is nevertheless broader than organized sport: findings about exercise cannot automatically be read as counts of club members or community-team players. [s1]\n\nA qualitative study published in 2018 explored women’s experiences in Tehran’s Khoramrudi neighbourhood through group discussions and interviews. Participants described companionship, encouragement and opportunities to meet people as reasons to exercise. Community participation therefore had a social dimension as well as an individual one. These findings describe the women interviewed, rather than every neighbourhood in Tehran. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "practical-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A nearby facility is only part of access",
        "content": "The Tehran participants identified several practical obstacles: costs, limited facilities, unsuitable session schedules, distance and insufficient information about available opportunities. They also discussed family responsibilities and the importance of supportive relationships. A facility can consequently exist without fitting someone’s available time or resources. [s1]\n\nMore recent interviews in Kermanshah add a perspective from western Iran. Women described restricted access to suitable spaces, limited opening times and concerns about safety. Some reported discomfort in public settings and frustration with the condition or availability of women’s spaces. These are documented experiences and perceptions; they do not establish that every public park or sports facility operates in the same way. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "national-and-local-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connecting local experience with national research",
        "content": "An analysis of Iran’s 2021 STEPwise surveillance examined reported barriers to physical activity among adult women and men. It separated personal, interpersonal, economic, cultural and environmental constraints. The study found differences by sex and place of residence, including more frequent reporting of environmental and cultural barriers in rural areas than urban areas. Such findings caution against treating the experience of an urban interview sample as a national template. [s2]\n\nThe survey also identified work commitments, family responsibilities and physical conditions among prominent reported barriers in the combined adult sample. Those findings should not be relabelled as women-only statistics. Nor does a cross-sectional survey show that a particular barrier caused someone to stop participating: it records responses and associations at a particular time. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-improvement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What meaningful improvement would involve",
        "content": "Read together, these studies suggest that evaluating community provision requires more than counting buildings. Relevant questions include whether women can reach a venue, afford participation, use it at workable times and feel comfortable there. This is an editorial synthesis of reported barriers, rather than an evaluation of a specific programme. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nThe Kermanshah research also highlights participants’ concerns about influence over sporting decisions. Its findings support listening to women’s accounts when assessing provision, while its local qualitative design limits national generalization. Community sport is best understood through both opportunities offered and the experiences of those expected to use them. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
